{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel were fruitful and swarmed.</b> Since Scripture already mentioned that Joseph died and all his brothers and all that generation, but the death of Jacob it did not mention — because Jacob did not die. And this merit — that they were the children of Israel — endured forever. Therefore it says, ״וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — by virtue of their being the children of Israel — ״פָּרוּ וַיִּשְׁרְצוּ״, etc. And what is written, ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and not ״בְּנֵי יַעֲקֹב״ — is because the name Jacob is corporeal and Israel is spiritual, for the materiality had already departed from Jacob." "<b>And they multiplied and grew strong exceedingly, exceedingly.</b> It should have said ״מְאֹד מְאֹד״; what is ״בִּמְאֹד״? Rather, Scripture wished to indicate that they also became wealthy in money, which is called ״מְאֹד״, as it is written (Deuteronomy 6:5) ״וּבְכָל מְאֹדֶךָ״. This — that it says ״וַיַּעַצְמוּ בִּמְאֹד״ — means in money, which is called ״מְאֹד״; in that ״מְאֹד״ they grew strong ״מְאֹד״. And therefore Pharaoh plotted to bring them to poverty, as it is written (Exodus 1:11) ״וַיָּשִׂימוּ עָלָיו שָׂרֵי מִסִּים״, for through the forced labor he would impose upon them they would come to poverty. And therefore Pharaoh was punished, for the building caused him to become impoverished, as it says (Exodus 1:11) ״וַיִּבֶן עָרֵי מִסְכְּנוֹת לְפַרְעֹה״, and as they said, of blessed memory (Yevamot 63a), ״הָעוֹסֵק בְּבִנְיָן מִתְמַסְכֵּן״ — 'one who engages in building becomes impoverished.'" "<b>And the land was filled with them.</b> It should have said, the land was from them — i.e., they filled the land; what is אֹתָם? Rather, the land itself caused them to be fruitful and to multiply, as we explained above on the verse ״וְיִדְגּוּ לָרֹב בְּקֶרֶב הָאָרֶץ״ (Genesis 48:16) — literally within it, as the Sages said (Exodus Rabbah 1:12) that the Egyptians would seek to kill them and a miracle was performed for them and they were absorbed into the ground, and the Egyptians would plow over them, and afterward they would sprout and emerge, as it says (Ezekiel 16:7) ״רְבָבָה כְּצֶמַח הַשָּׂדֶה נְתַתִּיךְ״, etc. And according to this, had the land not covered them, they would not have been so numerous — and if so, the land caused them to be so very filled. Therefore it did not say that they filled the land, but rather that the land filled them. And we may explain further the expression ״וַיִּשְׁרְצוּ״: after Joseph and all his brothers and all that generation died — the righteous ones who had protected them — from then on they became lowly like creeping things crawling in the dust in the eyes of the Egyptians; therefore it says ״וַיִּשְׁרְצוּ״."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:47.581485033Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}